#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba9b3820-2c88-588c-8149-74f4311067b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/d5927b5cc43ee1b5923b4d2aa4b4740e2bbc1c73b09b00525cb74767badc4808.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335678} {:answer/id #uuid "5193fae4-9ca9-5844-a705-8cd04c15abae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/787772f6d68af4df373cbfdd6b7a3cf3d313db7e03ecce36437f01068b738408.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335679} {:answer/id #uuid "233c301c-2915-5218-992c-ef218e3bd6ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/f5d7744d62f94b96b8bd1600ca7f5fc752d1f83c8c8928e740261e808cb9e07b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335680} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c00683-baab-5743-97b8-4035232dabdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/8d37dc99a006ba89bc6a874d251a065fc714b260072740206f9cc3692b08c604.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335681} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9acf66-d993-5a0e-87cd-0752623c602c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/4c9f5d5245a1a4aa33a0d474ff328a30a1c8820055551e3613bf7c9489c2a908.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e9acf66-d993-5a0e-87cd-0752623c602c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/4c9f5d5245a1a4aa33a0d474ff328a30a1c8820055551e3613bf7c9489c2a908.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335682} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3519f6d-17c0-5e34-805e-2f1cf9252804" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48cd65ad-908c-58d2-aca2-a54d10434503" :question/math-academy-id "q-127011" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $m(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $n(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $n(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $l(x)$  has jump discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $l(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)$  has removable discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cad6838-8a50-5f60-b93e-1abb69f91f52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\ln(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335685} {:answer/id #uuid "47853068-ebbd-5611-b478-88ec956464f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cad6838-8a50-5f60-b93e-1abb69f91f52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\ln(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335685} :answer-field/id #uuid "95722bcf-6190-5709-a0c8-07fc5783ca92" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12291ac2-c7d9-5f45-b59d-215869022663" :question/math-academy-id "q-345310" :question/problem "Find  the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x)=2{log}_{2}⁡x$ on the interval $[1,4].$\n\n$c=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[1,4]$ and differentiable on $(1,4),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (1,4)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=1$ and $b=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡4-2{log}_{2}⁡1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-0}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{log}_{2}⁡x)=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{2}{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ c\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ c & =\\frac{3}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdb1c625-222b-50cd-923b-8ac250832eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdb1c625-222b-50cd-923b-8ac250832eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335689} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fcde605-1d00-54f5-88c5-a6c705218b33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63d7f357-9d97-595c-b8ed-29148be14c18" :question/math-academy-id "q-345312" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x)={x}^{4}-x$ on the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$c=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem.  \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (-1,1)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=-1$ and $b=1$ into the above gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(1)-f(-1)}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{({1}^{4}-1)-((-1)^{4}-(-1))}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}-x)=4{x}^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=4{c}^{3}-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{c}^{3}-1 & =-1 \\\\ c & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccf8c6d7-3823-5e52-8706-77bdea563219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062892} {:answer/id #uuid "e637cb87-bc66-51c6-8108-ce814d4b3146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062893} {:answer/id #uuid "e898f5bf-94ad-57a5-839b-91f8a320cadf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062894} {:answer/id #uuid "30426d13-3295-55c4-8dae-890b1d9565a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062895} {:answer/id #uuid "68ab435f-a152-5604-94f1-896a925cccee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e637cb87-bc66-51c6-8108-ce814d4b3146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062893} :answer-field/id #uuid "509c46cd-b6e5-517c-a82d-9bac09dc7ddc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9e7b61b7-5224-5508-b07f-2f649466a990" :question/math-academy-id "q-48352" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5]?$\n\n- $f(x)={x}^{3}$\n- $g(x)=|x|$\n- $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{3}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0$ but this point does not lie inside $(1,5).$\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ is not defined at $x=1$, so it is not continuous at $x=1$, which means it is not continuous on $[1,5].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53fbe4c2-4144-5047-a7e4-d1e31797cade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335692} {:answer/id #uuid "65c70c6b-e499-5d0d-845a-7b1613d22bfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335693} {:answer/id #uuid "6991e8e7-3eaf-54a6-852f-d016d2258f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335694} {:answer/id #uuid "3baf9a3c-f034-580c-bfba-391e0726a331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335695} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a3f064-6051-5783-a35b-84a5a7578785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65c70c6b-e499-5d0d-845a-7b1613d22bfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335693} :answer-field/id #uuid "83789283-1ace-5c05-87c9-c48f6c96e163" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d6a5882-b479-5d6b-aea9-d1ab879c1fe4" :question/math-academy-id "q-77865" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-4,4]?$\n\n- $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}$\n- $g(x)={2}^{1/{x}^{2}}$\n- $h(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-4,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[-4,4]$ and differentiable on $(-4,4).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-4,4]$ and differentiable on $(-4,4).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)={2}^{1/{x}^{2}}$ is not defined at $x=0,$ hence, it is not continuous at \n$x=0,$ and so it is not continuous on $[-4,4].$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}$ is continuous on $[-4,\\infty )$ and differentiable on $(-4,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-4,4]$ and differentiable on $(-4,4).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions I and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-4,4].$"}]}
